meta {
  name: Update Other Users Checkin (404)
  type: http
  seq: 12
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/checkins/{{checkinId}}
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "message": "Trying to edit someone else's checkin."
  }
}

docs {
  Ownership check. To run it: create a checkin as one account, then run Create
  Test User (which recaptures accessToken) and send this against the first
  account's checkinId.

  Expect 404 {"error": "Checkin not found"} — deliberately NOT 403.

  A 403 would confirm the id is real, which hands an attacker a way to probe
  which checkin ids exist. The update is scoped by owner in the WHERE clause,
  so a checkin belonging to someone else returns no rows and falls through to
  the same 404 as one that never existed.

  Before auth this route had no ownership check at all: anyone who knew a uuid
  could edit any checkin.
}
